{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Intermittent severe frontal headaches associated with feeling hot, palpitations, shortness of breath, bilateral hand tingling, decreased appetite and 5 lb unintentional weight loss; decreased libido.\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating-alleviating factors): Started around time of job change (several weeks to months). Headaches are intermittent, often during work (meetings or at computer), frontal, sometimes with mild nausea; tingling in both hands described as \"quick picking\", often when entering elevator or at work; palpitations ~once/day (maybe more), feeling hot/short of breath while sitting at desk, decreased appetite, unintentional 5 lb weight loss; symptoms described as affecting work; triggers often work-related/stressful situations. No clear alleviating factors reported. Severity interfering with work; stress level 7–8/10.\n- Past medical history: Patient reports \"hyperthyroidism\" and takes thyroxine (possible inconsistency between hyperthyroid diagnosis and thyroxine medication).\n- Medications/allergies: Medication: thyroxine (patient reports). No known drug allergies.\n- Family/social history: Works as a lawyer in London; high work stress; drinks alcohol ~once/week (couple of glasses); denies tobacco and recreational drugs; no family history documented in transcript.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: None recorded in transcript (no vitals, no physical exam performed yet).\n- Labs/imaging: None reported; doctor plans thyroid blood tests (thyroxine/TSH) and physical exam including cardiac and respiratory examination.\n\nAdditional relevant points: 35-year-old male; symptoms began after job change; denies stimulant or illicit drug use; no prior frequent headaches reported before this illness; no leg paresthesias; patient willing to be seen for in-person exam and labs.",
